  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nStalled frontal boundary remains across the south, while a plume\nof low level moisture extends from central to northeastern\nportions. Overall abundant instability will be found across the\nsouthern half of the state this evening, possibly becoming \nuncapped, at least briefly. Shear will also remain high tonight\nas a decent jet remains aloft. The limiting factor will be the\nlift mechanism for tonight. Surface boundary combined with a\nweak upper level wave could be enough to produce at least some \nisolated thunderstorms. Although the better lift and moisture \nmay be to our south tonight. SPC has maintained a Marginal Risk \nfor most of southern ND through tonight to account for this \nisolated conditional severe weather threat. Otherwise look for \nlows in the 50s tonight. Increased low level moisture combined \nwith some light winds could bring some fog tonight, especially \nin the southwest. More showers and thunderstorms could return on\nTuesday as this boundary lingers. Inverted surface trough moves\nmore into western ND Tuesday afternoon. Meanwhile high amounts \nof instability and shear also look to be found. Instability may \neven be uncapped by mid afternoon Tuesday. CAMs consistently \nshowing storms firing ahead of the surface trough as a result \nand moving from west to east Tuesday afternoon and evening. \nShear and instability will be high enough for golf ball sized \nhail and wind gusts up to 70 mph. Depending how the shear vector\nsets up, multi cluster to supercell storm mode is possible. If \nsupercell storm mode materializes then perhaps some larger hail \nis possible. Tornado threat both tonight through Tuesday night \nalso looks low as LCLs are high and hodographs are not quite \ncurved enough. Overall SPC has a Day 2 Marginal to Slight Risk \nfor Tuesday to account for a potentially more widespread severe\nweather threat. Biggest uncertainty being if the surface trough\nprovides enough lift for thunderstorm initiation. Otherwise \nlook for near normal temperatures in the 80s on Tuesday. Lows \nTuesday night will be in the 50s. Light winds and high humidity \nvalues could also see patchy fog return Tuesday night. \n\nWednesday through Friday, cooler temperatures look to move into\nthe area along with near daily chances for showers and\nthunderstorms especially across the west and south. CSU-MLP\nshowing some low chances for isolated severe weather on \nWednesday, as shear remains high yet expect lower instability. \nAfter Wednesday CSU-MLP not showing much for severe weather \nchances this week. Today's models continue to show a more robust\nwave and surface low moving through sometime Thursday through \nFriday. Increased southerly flow ahead of this wave could also \nbring increased PWAT values and perhaps QPF. NBM even showing a \n30 to 60 percent chance of at least an inch of liquid in a 24 \nhour period Thursday night through Friday across much of western\nand some of central ND during this time period. Isolated shower\nand thunderstorm chances then look to linger into the weekend, \nalthough depending on track of a surface high this could further\nlower these chances. Temperatures may increase slightly yet \nstill remain below normal.",
